<section class="block-section story">
  <span class="section-tag">Story</span>
  <h2>What today is about</h2>
  <p class="sub">Full work block. The Dossier is due at the start of next block, so this is the last class time you get on it. Paired defense rehearsal in the second half.</p>
  <p>The first half is yours to work. Finish what is not finished in your <a href="/units/unit-0/assessment-source-dossier/">Source Dossier</a>, and fix the parts you already know are thin. The second half is rehearsal: paired peer-talk where each of you takes a turn defending your dossier while the other plays panelist. Twice. Then a 30-second whole-class share of your strongest pitch.</p>
  <p>Rehearsal isn't optional. The first defense you give in Block 7 has to be at least your second defense, not your first. Make your mistakes today, with one partner, so you don't make them next class in front of the panel. Your partner today is doing you a favor.</p>
  <figure class="content-figure">
    <img src="/assets/img/u0/u0-five-moves.svg" alt="Five-step strip for answering questions under pressure: one, acknowledge (take the question seriously); two, restate (say it back in your own words); three, answer (one claim, not a speech); four, cite (name the evidence it stands on); five, check (does that answer it).">
    <figcaption>The pattern you'll rehearse today and use in every defense this year.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="block-section concepts">
  <span class="section-tag">Concepts</span>
  <h2>Before you start the work</h2>
  <p class="sub">Start with the Watch: projected for everyone. The Read is the defending guide, and the Interact is used live during rehearsal in pairs.</p>
  <div class="entry-grid">
    <div class="entry-card placeholder">
      <span class="placeholder-tag">Placeholder</span>
      <div class="entry-title">Watch (~6 min)</div>
      <div>TBD: Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Ignash demos a defense answer to one likely panelist question, modeling the answer pattern (acknowledge → restate → answer → cite → check).</div>
      <div class="entry-meta">Whole-class, projected.</div>
    </div>
    <a class="entry-card" href="/foundations/defending-your-work/" style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit;">
      <div class="entry-title">Read (~8 min)</div>
      <div>Defending Your Work: the 5 question types panelists ask, and the 5 moves you can make in response. The whole-class share is graded against this taxonomy.</div>
      <div class="entry-meta">Real link · Foundations</div>
    </a>
    <div class="entry-card">
      <div class="entry-title">Interact</div>
      <div>The Defense Observation Guide, in Classroom: the one-page sheet the panelist-partner uses to give specific feedback (rather than "that was good"). Printed copies at every table. <a href="/foundations/defending-your-work/#answer-pattern">The answer pattern is on the site</a>; your partner checks each answer against it.</div>
      <div class="entry-meta">Pairs · used live during rehearsal.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: choose one path</h2>
  <p class="sub">Work time runs the first 35 minutes. Rehearsal runs the next 30, and the choice board below is how you rehearse it. Whole-class share is the last 15.</p>
  <div class="callout activity">
    <div class="callout-title">Use this block on the thing you are avoiding</div>
    <p>Most people spend a work block polishing the section they already like. The Limitations of your AI source and the cross-source synthesis are where the marks are, and they are the two people leave until last. Start there.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="choice-intro">
    <strong>Learning Intention:</strong> {{ learningIntention | safe }}
  </div>
  <div class="choice-board">
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">A</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Paired defense rehearsal (3-min Q&A each)</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">Pair up. Partner A defends 3 min while Partner B asks 2-3 panelist questions. Switch. Use the observation guide for specific feedback. Default for most students.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Pairs · Default</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">B</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Solo rehearsal with partner-as-audience</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">If you want to rehearse the 5-min talk uninterrupted first, do that; partner listens silently and gives feedback after. Then swap. Good if your nerves benefit from a quiet first run.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Pairs · Choice</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">C</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Self-review using the rubric</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">For students writing the essay defense instead of speaking it: self-review your draft against the <a href="/foundations/the-rubric/">rubric</a>. Mark which strand each section serves. It goes in with the dossier at the start of next block.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Solo · Choice</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">D</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Anticipating-questions worksheet</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">If oral rehearsal is high-anxiety, work the anticipating-questions worksheet instead: list 5 questions a panelist would likely ask and draft your answer to each. Bring this to your Block 7 defense.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Solo · Practice</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  {% include "partials/notebook-cue.njk" %}
</section>

<section class="block-section show">
  <span class="section-tag">Show</span>
  <h2>What you're submitting today</h2>
  <p class="sub">Two short things. The dossier itself is not due today; it is due at the start of next block.</p>
  <div class="submission-callout">
    <div class="label">Rehearsal peer-feedback note + progress check</div>
    <p>The peer-feedback note (one paragraph from your partner, written on the observation guide) goes to Classroom by end of class. Underneath it, write one line: which section of your dossier is still unfinished going into next block. Name the section. "Nearly done" tells us nothing, and this line is how Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Ignash know who to find before the defenses.</p>
    {% set submit = { id: "u0-b6", text: "Submit via Google Classroom" } %}{% include "partials/submit-link.njk" %}
  </div>
  <p class="rubric-meta">
    <strong>Rubric link:</strong> The defense is the C strand on the Source Dossier rubric. See the <a href="/foundations/the-rubric/">rubric</a>: Communication scores are about clarity AND responsiveness under questioning.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="block-section reflect">
  <span class="section-tag">Reflect</span>
  <h2>One question before you leave</h2>
  <p class="sub">Three to five minutes. Your answer is saved to your reflection journal, where you can read back everything you have written this year.</p>
  <p class="sub">Use it to plan for Block 7.</p>
  {% include "partials/reflect.njk" %}
</section>
